hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to the  edition of ask Evan today I'm gonna  answer questions from one of my Facebook  readers in the Dean who wrote insane  hello  I don't know what to do because my  family doesn't approve of me becoming an  entrepreneur and taking risks can I have  some advice so thanks for the question a  Dean really started to hear about that  situation and it's actually quite common  in general you know the safe way to live  your life is to go to school get your  first job become an accountant or a  doctor or lawyer and you know go through  that path and I think the world kind of  leads us towards those paths and it can  be tough when everyone around you is on  that path and it's hard to break free in  terms of what to do with your parents my  suggestion would be to start small so  you know you don't want to risk that  relationship and what I would try to do  is build your business up on the side  part-time I don't know if you're going  to school or not or if you're in the  workforce or not but start by  moonlighting separates or something  small on the side build up something to  first see if you like it right see if  you like being an entrepreneur because  it's hard and it sounds exciting but  once you're into it the glamour kind of  goes away and you're you're stuck  running this business so make sure  something you really like and a great  way to do it is to start part-time see  if you can start making some money on it  see if you if you find value in it see  if you're helping people see if you're  getting good feedback from people and  see if it's something you want to  continue doing or not and I think at  least with some parents if they see that  you're really excited about something  and that you're helping people it's  easier to to get their approval and they  get them to say yes you know I don't  know how helpful they'll be ongoing but  that part is a little easier you know  one one common thing about parents is  they want to see their kids happy and  successful and so they're I'm sure  worried about you that you're gonna not  be successful with your business and not  be able to you know have your your place  I guess in society so seeing that you  are happy  that you are helping people even if it's  not making a lot of money yet that's a  good start so that's what I would do at  the beginning as it starts to grow and  you think you're getting you know  there's something you want to continue  doing and you're getting a lot of fun  out of it you get a lot of value helping  a lot of people with it I try to  negotiate something so a great example  is with Michael Dell so if you look at  Dell store we have on the website but  long story short he was a university  student I think as University of Austin  and he started his his business making  these computers in his dorm room and it  was growing and became you know he  needed to expand beyond it and it  started getting in the way of his  studies and his parents wanted him to  continue on and I think become a doctor  and he made a deal with them to say  listen I know you want me to go and  finish school and you know become the  doctor and finished my education but I  really want to be an entrepreneur and  here's my proposal to you I'm gonna take  one summer I'm gonna take the next  summer off I'm not gonna do an  internship I'm not gonna you know go get  a job I'm gonna take the summer off and  I'm gonna work on my business and if I  can grow it to the point where can  sustain myself and keep it going then  I'm gonna run that business and not go  back to school and so it was a trial so  it's easier for them to say yes to  something on a small scale and see if it  works and if you can come back with data  to say hey look we're growing I'm making  money I'm adding a lot of value I'm a  positive member of this of our community  in society then it's easier to get their  approval and to keep going and to you  know continue to build this business I  know it's still tough and at the end of  the day if you really want to do it and  they don't want you to do it you have to  make that decision and figure out how  much is that going to impact your  relationship with them and how important  is it versus starting your own business  you know at the end of the day most  parents love their kids and want to see  them be successful even if it goes  against what they originally had in mind  you know I think a lot of parents have  have a game plan for their kids and they  almost never follow it so again what I  would start doing is start part time  continue the path you're on but start  part time see if you like it  see if you're able to help people make  some money if you're able to get it to a  certain point where you think this could  really take off they're trying to  negotiate a little bit of time off if  it's the summer or half a year or  whatever it is focus on building your  business and then you know if you're  able to achieve that success keep on  going in my own case this is a quick  example I went to university I finished  my degree undergrad Bachelor of Commerce  at University of Toronto I started my  business the first one when I was in  university so I was in third year  university and when I graduated I had  job offers and successful companies and  you know banks investment banks and  salting companies and they were  interesting you know and it's it's  seductive it's what all my friends were  doing at the time were in the university  in my classes with me and I thought once  I get on that treadmill it's really hard  to get off you know if you're 40 years  old or 50 years old and you've been  doing this for a while  it's really really hard to jump off that  corporate treadmill and do your own  thing the risks are a lot higher if you  have a family and kids and so on and so  that I thought to myself at least the  time to do this is now and I told myself  and luckily my parents were very I had  their support I didn't have to worry  about that it was more what I wanted to  do and I told myself I'm gonna take a  year and try this thing and if I can't  make it work then I can always go back  at those jobs again in a year but I'm  gonna take a year and try it out and see  what I can make of it and I took that  year and I never went back so that's my  recommendation to you so hopefully this  helped for those of you watching if you  liked the video please give it a thumbs  up below always gives me more motivation  and encouragement to do more videos for  you and I'd love to hear we have to  think if you wanna leave a comment below  comment about this video or give a  question about your own company or  business I'd love to try to answer it  for you I always read those and I  appreciate them when I when they come in  so thank you and we'll see you on the  next episode  you
 

